["<aside class=\"callout why\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Why it matters</div><p>Ducks come in fast, in poor light, and some have their own bag limits. You must name the bird before you pull the trigger, from the side, from below and in the air.</p></aside><ul><li>Each screen shows one species group: drake (male), hen (female) and, where we found one, a flight shot.</li></ul><ul><li>In fall, many drakes are still in drab <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Eclipse plumage\">eclipse plumage</button> early in the season and look like hens <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Learn the wing patch, the <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Speculum\">speculum</button>: it shows on both sexes and in flight <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Learn the shape too: <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Puddle duck\">puddle ducks</button> jump straight up off the water; <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Diver\">divers</button> run along the surface to take off <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Photos are illustrations. The written field marks are the source of truth.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/wigeon-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Drake, Reifel sanctuary, Ladner, BC. White crown, green band behind the eye.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake, Reifel sanctuary, Ladner, BC. White crown, green band behind the eye. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Judy Gallagher, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_Wigeon_(male)_-_Anas_americana,_Reifel_Sanctuary,_Ladner,_British_Columbia.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/wigeon-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Hen. Grey head, rusty sides, small blue grey bill with a black tip.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Hen. Grey head, rusty sides, small blue grey bill with a black tip. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Mdf, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anas-americana-004.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wigeon-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Drake dropping in, South Dakota. White shoulder patches.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake dropping in, South Dakota. White shoulder patches. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:American_Wigeon_in_Flight_Huron_Wetland_Management_District_(53659241482).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Drake: white forehead and crown, so hunters call it baldpate <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Both sexes: short blue grey bill with a black tip <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. In flight the drake shows a big white patch on the front of the wing <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/green-winged-teal-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Drake. Chestnut head, green swoosh, white bar on the side.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake. Chestnut head, green swoosh, white bar on the side. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Rhododendrites, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Green-winged_teal_drake_(83852).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/green-winged-teal-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Hen, California. Very small brown duck, small dark bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Hen, California. Very small brown duck, small dark bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Mike's Birds from Riverside, CA, US, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Female_Green-winged_Teal-_Bolsa_Chica_Wetlands_(4412427221).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-gwt-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Flock in flight, Wyoming. Small, fast, bunched.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Flock in flight, Wyoming. Small, fast, bunched. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service Headquarters, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Seedskadee_National_Wildlife_Refuge_(53485904507).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/blue-winged-teal-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Look alike: blue winged teal drake in flight. White face crescent, pale blue forewing.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Look alike: blue winged teal drake in flight. White face crescent, pale blue forewing. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Frank Schulenburg, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blue-winged_teal_at_Sacramento_National_Wildlife_Refuge-5326.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>The smallest dabbling duck in BC. Flocks twist and turn in tight bunches <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Blue winged teal mostly leave BC early in fall (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/gadwall-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Drake. Plain grey, black rear end, dark bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake. Plain grey, black rear end, dark bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Department of the Interior. U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service. National Conservation Training Center. 10/1997-8888, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gadwall_Drake_-_DPLA_-_3a0518ab1519a6044a5182c72add2cba.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/gadwall-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Hen, North Dakota. Orange sided bill, white wing patch.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Hen, North Dakota. Orange sided bill, white wing patch. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hen_Gadwall_at_J_Clark_Salyer_NWR_(29622615338).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/gadwall-drake-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Drake in flight, Oregon. Small square white patch on the back of the wing.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake in flight, Oregon. Small square white patch on the back of the wing. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Oregon Department of Fish &amp; Wildlife, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gadwall_male_myatt_odfw_(7591220124).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>The only puddle duck with a white speculum <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>A gadwall hen looks like a mallard hen. Check the wing: white, not blue <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/shoveler-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Drake, North Dakota. Spoon bill, green head, white chest, rusty sides.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake, North Dakota. Spoon bill, green head, white chest, rusty sides. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drake_Northern_Shoveler_at_J_Clark_Salyer_NWR_(42589430525).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/shoveler-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Hen. Mottled brown with a long spoon bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Hen. Mottled brown with a long spoon bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Northern_shoveler_(Spatula_clypeata)_female_Marken.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-shoveler-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Drake in flight. Spoon bill, pale blue forewing, rusty belly.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake in flight. Spoon bill, pale blue forewing, rusty belly. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: mourad-harzallah, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_Northern_shoveler_(Spatula_clypeata)_flying.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-shoveler-flock.jpg\" alt=\"Flock taking off, Texas.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Flock taking off, Texas. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Steve Hillebrand, U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Anas_clypeata_flying_(cropped).jpeg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>The bill is longer than the head and wide at the tip <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>. Flies with the bill pointed slightly down (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/wood-duck-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Drake. Green crest, white chin straps, red eye.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake. Green crest, white chin straps, red eye. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Rhododendrites, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wood_duck_drake_vocalizing_in_Central_Park_(86815).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/wood-duck-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Hen. White teardrop around the eye.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Hen. White teardrop around the eye. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Russ, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wood_duck_hen,_mill_pond_(35953689361).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-wood-duck-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Drake in flight, Texas. Long square tail, head up.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Drake in flight, Texas. Long square tail, head up. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Manjith Kainickara from Dallas, Texas, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aix_sponsa_-Texas,_USA_-flying-8.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Lives on wooded ponds, beaver ponds and sloughs with trees <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>In flight: long square tail, head held up, bill pointed down (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/ring-necked-duck-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Ring necked drake, New Mexico. Peaked head, white ring on the bill, white spur.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ring necked drake, New Mexico. Peaked head, white ring on the bill, white spur. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Chuck Homler d/b/a Focus On Wildlife, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ring-necked_duck_(Aythya_collaris)_-_Las_Cruces_-_New_Mexico_-_USA_(cropped).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/ring-necked-duck-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Ring necked hen. White eye ring, pale band on the bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ring necked hen. White eye ring, pale band on the bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Polinova, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ring-necked_Duck_Female.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/lesser-scaup-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Scaup drake. Black at both ends, white sides, grey back.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Scaup drake. Black at both ends, white sides, grey back. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Chuck Homler d/b/a Focus On Wildlife, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Drake_Lesser_Scaup_(Aythya_affinis)_Barengat_Inlet,_New_Jersey,_USA.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/lesser-scaup-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Scaup hen. Bold white patch at the bill base.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Scaup hen. Bold white patch at the bill base. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Chuck Homler d/b/a Focus On Wildlife, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Hen_Lesser_Scaup_(Aythya_affinis),_Barnegat_Inlet,_New_Jersey,_USA.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-scaup-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Scaup in flight, South Dakota. Dark front, pale back, fast wingbeats.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Scaup in flight, South Dakota. Dark front, pale back, fast wingbeats. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lesser_Scaup_Flying_Lake_Andes_National_Wildlife_Refuge_South_Dakota_(52780921182).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Ring necked drake: black back. Scaup drake: grey back <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Ring necked ducks like small ponds and marshes; scaup like big lakes and the coast (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/bufflehead-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Bufflehead drake, California. Tiny, white wedge on the back of the head.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bufflehead drake, California. Tiny, white wedge on the back of the head. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Mike's Birds from Riverside, CA, US, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Male_Bufflehead_(4239833567).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/bufflehead-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Bufflehead hen. One white oval on the cheek.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bufflehead hen. One white oval on the cheek. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Mdf, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucephala-albeola-010.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/common-goldeneye-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Common goldeneye drake. Round white spot, yellow eye.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Common goldeneye drake. Round white spot, yellow eye. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: CheepShot, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Winthrop_Harbor_(13334802213).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/barrows-goldeneye-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Barrow's goldeneye drake, Wyoming. White crescent, more black on the sides.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Barrow's goldeneye drake, Wyoming. White crescent, more black on the sides. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain-Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barrow%27s_Goldeneye_Drake_Seedskadee_NWR_(16656643482).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/common-goldeneye-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Common goldeneye hen. Brown head, yellow eye.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Common goldeneye hen. Brown head, yellow eye. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Charles J. Sharp, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_goldeneye_(Bucephala_clangula_americana)_female_Prince%27s_Island.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/barrows-goldeneye-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Barrow's goldeneye hen. Mostly orange yellow bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Barrow's goldeneye hen. Mostly orange yellow bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Pacific Southwest Region USFWS from Sacramento, US, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Barrow%27s_Goldeneye_(female)_(6881754634).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Goldeneye wings whistle in flight <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Goldeneyes have their own limit inside the duck bag: 2 a day, Barrow's and common together (99%, page 12, 2 October 2026 edition).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-bufflehead-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Bufflehead drake, California. Big white wing patches, white head wedge.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Bufflehead drake, California. Big white wing patches, white head wedge. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Bill Bouton from San Luis Obispo, CA, USA, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bucephala_albeola_-San_Luis_Obispo,_California,_USA_-flying-8.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-goldeneye-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Common goldeneye hen. Brown head, white wing patches.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Common goldeneye hen. Brown head, white wing patches. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Casey Helton, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Common_Goldeneye_flying_(54278907808).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Both fly fast and low over open water, often in small groups (Tip).</li><li>Black and white small diver with a white head wedge: bufflehead. Bigger, with a round white face spot: goldeneye drake <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/canvasback-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Canvasback drake, Maryland. Long sloping black bill, rusty head, white back.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Canvasback drake, Maryland. Long sloping black bill, rusty head, white back. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Judy Gallagher, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canvasback_(male)_-_Aythya_valisineria,_Cambridge,_Maryland.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/canvasback-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Canvasback hen. Pale, same sloping profile.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Canvasback hen. Pale, same sloping profile. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Judy Gallagher, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canvasback_(female)_-_Aythya_valisineria,_Oakley_Street,_Cambridge,_Maryland.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/redhead-drake.jpg\" alt=\"Redhead drake. Round head, grey back, blue bill with a black tip.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Redhead drake. Round head, grey back, blue bill with a black tip. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Rhododendrites, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Redhead_in_Central_Park_(15503).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/redhead-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Redhead hen, Ontario. Plain brown, round head.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Redhead hen, Ontario. Plain brown, round head. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Mykola Swarnyk, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Aythya_americana_f_Ontario.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-redhead-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Redheads in flight, South Dakota. Round red heads, black chests.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Redheads in flight, South Dakota. Round red heads, black chests. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Redheads_Lake_Andes_National_Wildlife_Refuge_South_Dakota_(53921083806).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Profile tells them apart: canvasback forehead runs straight into the bill; redhead has a round head <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Canvasback has its own limit inside the duck bag: 4 a day (99%, page 12).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/divers-taking-off.jpg\" alt=\"Diving ducks running across the water to take off, South Dakota.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Diving ducks running across the water to take off, South Dakota. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Waterfowl_Lake_Andes_National_Wildlife_Refuge_South_Dakota_(52781566934).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/mallards-flying.jpg\" alt=\"Mallards taking off. Every bird shows the blue wing patch.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Mallards taking off. Every bird shows the blue wing patch. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Jocelyn Anderson, CC BY 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mallard_Ducks_Taking_Off_Over_A_Lake_(185682473).jpeg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/mallards-flushing.jpg\" alt=\"A flock flushing off a wetland, Nebraska. Pick one bird and name it.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>A flock flushing off a wetland, Nebraska. Pick one bird and name it. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: USFWS Mountain Prairie, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Rainwater_Basin_Wetland_Management_District_(32227957367).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/pintail-hen.jpg\" alt=\"Pintail hen in flight. Long neck, slim body, pointed tail.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Pintail hen in flight. Long neck, slim body, pointed tail. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Frank Schulenburg, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Female_northern_pintail_in_flight-1827.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>Puddle ducks spring straight up. Divers patter across the water first <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Never shoot into a flock. Pick one bird you have named (Tip).</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/canada-goose.jpg\" alt=\"Canada goose. Long neck, black head, white chin strap.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Canada goose. Long neck, black head, white chin strap. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Joe Ravi, CC BY-SA 3.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canada_Goose_(Branta_Canadensis).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/cackling-goose.jpg\" alt=\"Cackling goose. Same colours, mallard sized, stubby bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cackling goose. Same colours, mallard sized, stubby bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: VJAnderson, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cackling_Goose_02.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/cackling-and-canada-geese.jpg\" alt=\"Cackling geese with a Canada goose (right). Compare neck and bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Cackling geese with a Canada goose (right). Compare neck and bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Andy Reago &amp; Chrissy McClarren, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cackling_Goose_(31714908975).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-canada-goose-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Canada goose in flight, Burnaby Lake, BC.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Canada goose in flight, Burnaby Lake, BC. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Image taken by Alan D. Wilson, and modified by Diliff (cropped and noise reduction applied)., CC BY-SA 2.5, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canada_goose_flight_cropped_and_NR.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>A <button class=\"term\" data-term=\"Cackling goose\">cackling goose</button> is a separate species from the Canada goose, not a young one <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">85%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Both sit in the dark goose bag in BC; check your district's goose limits (<span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> for your area).</li></ul><figure class=\"video\"><video controls preload=\"none\" playsinline src=\"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/transcoded/a/a6/Canada_geese_%28Branta_canadensis%29_in_flight.webm/Canada_geese_%28Branta_canadensis%29_in_flight.webm.480p.vp9.webm\"></video><figcaption>Canada geese in flight: long black neck, white chin strap. <span class=\"credit\">Video: tuchodi, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canada_geese_(Branta_canadensis)_in_flight.webm\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a>. Needs internet.</span></figcaption></figure>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/snow-goose.jpg\" alt=\"Snow goose. White, black wing tips, pink bill with a dark grin patch.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snow goose. White, black wing tips, pink bill with a dark grin patch. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Paul Danese, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20250118_snow_goose_barnes_boat_launch_PD000302.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/ross-goose.jpg\" alt=\"Ross's goose. Smaller, stubby bill, no grin patch.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Ross's goose. Smaller, stubby bill, no grin patch. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Andrew C, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ross%27s_Goose_(Chen_rossii)_(23108182770).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/snow-and-ross-geese.jpg\" alt=\"Snow (left) and Ross's (right) side by side.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snow (left) and Ross's (right) side by side. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Nomdeploom, CC0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snow_Goose_Ross_Goose_comparison.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-snow-geese-skagit.jpg\" alt=\"Snow geese over the Skagit Valley, Washington. Black wing tips.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snow geese over the Skagit Valley, Washington. Black wing tips. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Eric Frommer, CC BY-SA 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Skagit_Valley_Snow_Geese.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-snow-geese-flock.jpg\" alt=\"Snow geese lifting off, California. Dark birds are the blue form.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Snow geese lifting off, California. Dark birds are the blue form. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Frank Schulenburg, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Snow_geese_flying_up_at_Sacramento_National_Wildlife_Refuge.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><ul><li>White geese show black wing tips in flight. A swan is all white and much bigger <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul>","<div class=\"gallery\"><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-loon.jpg\" alt=\"Common loon. Dagger bill, checkered back.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Common loon. Dagger bill, checkered back. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: John Picken from Chicago, USA, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Gavia_immer_-Minocqua,_Wisconsin,_USA_-swimming-8.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-western-grebe.jpg\" alt=\"Western grebes. Long white neck, thin yellow bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Western grebes. Long white neck, thin yellow bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Ron Knight from Seaford, East Sussex, United Kingdom, CC BY 2.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Western_Grebe_(Aechmophorus_occidentalis)_(20357238151).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-horned-grebe.jpg\" alt=\"Horned grebe in winter. Small, black cap, white cheeks.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Horned grebe in winter. Small, black cap, white cheeks. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: Chuck Homler, Focus On Wildlife, CC BY-SA 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Horned_Grebe_(Podiceps_auritus)_in_winter_plumage,_Scarborough_Marsh,_Maine,_USA.png\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-trumpeter-swan.jpg\" alt=\"Trumpeter swan. Huge, all white, black bill.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Trumpeter swan. Huge, all white, black bill. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: TRinaud, CC BY 4.0, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Trumpeter_swan_(Cygnus_buccinator)_2023_2.jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure><figure class=\"photo\"><img src=\"photos/g-tundra-swans-flight.jpg\" alt=\"Tundra swans in flight. No black wing tips.\" loading=\"lazy\"><figcaption>Tundra swans in flight. No black wing tips. <span class=\"credit\">Photo: U.S. Fish and Wildlife Service - Midwest Region, Public domain, <a href=\"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tundra_Swan_(26260317135).jpg\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">source</a></span></figcaption></figure></div><aside class=\"callout warn\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Study aid only. The official regulations are the law.</div></aside><ul><li>Loons and grebes have no open season. They are protected <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li></ul>","<ul><li>Swans: no open season in BC (<span class=\"verify\">VERIFY</span> in the ECCC, Environment and Climate Change Canada, BC migratory bird summary). An all white bird bigger than a goose, with no black wing tips, is a swan <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li></ul><ul><li>Harlequin duck is legal in BC, but only 2 a day inside the duck bag (99%, page 12).</li></ul>","<aside class=\"callout rule\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Grandpa's rule (wisdom, not law)</div><p>If you cannot name it, it is not a duck yet. Let it go and wait for the next one.</p></aside>","<aside class=\"callout mistake\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Common mistakes</div><ul><li>Shooting at a dark shape at first light before you can see the wing patch.</li><li>Calling every small white duck a bufflehead. Check the head pattern.</li><li>Shooting a grebe or a loon that dove and came up near the decoys.</li></ul></aside>","<aside class=\"callout field\"><div class=\"callout-t\">Do this in the field</div><ul><li>Sit at a marsh with binoculars for an hour. Name each duck: species, drake or hen.</li><li>Watch how each one takes off. Jump up, or run along the water?</li><li>Count how many goldeneyes and canvasbacks you would have in a bag of 8.</li></ul></aside>","<ul><li>BC <a href=\"https://www2.gov.bc.ca/assets/gov/sports-recreation-arts-and-culture/outdoor-recreation/fishing-and-hunting/hunting/regulations/hunting-trapping-synopsis.pdf\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Hunting and Trapping Regulations Synopsis 2026 to 2028, 2 October 2026 edition</a>, read directly: duck daily aggregate bag of 8, with limits on pintail, canvasback, goldeneye and harlequin, page 12. Checked 2026-10-02 <span class=\"cert c-hi\" title=\"Certainty\">99%</span>.</li><li>Field marks follow the Ducks, Geese and Sea ducks sessions in this app, and the <a href=\"https://www.allaboutbirds.org/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Cornell Lab All About Birds</a> species guides <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">80%</span>.</li><li>Loons and grebes have no open season: see the Ducks session <span class=\"cert c-mid\" title=\"Certainty\">90%</span>.</li><li>Photos: Wikimedia Commons, public domain or Creative Commons. Credits under each photo.</li></ul>"]